    { "front": "Name the non-dihydropyridine and the starred dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers from this lecture.", "back": "Non-DHP: verapamil and diltiazem (oral and I.V.). DHP oral: nifedipine and amlodipine. DHP I.V. only: nicardipine and clevidipine. Nimodipine is an oral-only DHP reserved for subarachnoid hemorrhage.", "tag": "Glossary" },
    { "front": "What are the class pharmacokinetic properties of the CCBs?", "back": "Good oral absorption but reduced bioavailability from hepatic first-pass effect; high plasma protein binding; many are P-glycoprotein substrates; CYP-mediated metabolism especially CYP3A4; half-lives ranging from about 1.3 to 64 hours. Extended-release forms reduce dosing frequency of short-acting agents.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Why are immediate-release, short-half-life dihydropyridines not recommended?", "back": "Within each dosing interval they cause a rapid drop in blood pressure, oscillations in blood pressure control, and concurrent surges in sympathetic reflex activity (reflex tachycardia). Extended-release nifedipine and amlodipine avoid the peaks.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Trace the smooth muscle contraction pathway that CCBs interrupt.", "back": "Calcium influx through L-type channels triggers calcium-induced calcium release; the calcium-calmodulin complex activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK); MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chains; myosin and actin interact; the muscle contracts. CCBs block step one, producing vasodilation.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Where do CCBs bind the channel, and which channel state do they prefer?", "back": "They bind a site on the main pore-forming alpha-1 subunit, acting from the inner side of the membrane, and bind more effectively to open and inactivated channels than to resting ones. Binding reduces the frequency of channel opening in response to depolarization.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "How do verapamil and diltiazem differ from dihydropyridines in dose-dependent effects?", "back": "Verapamil and diltiazem decrease the channel's rate of recovery, block is enhanced as stimulation frequency increases (verapamil > diltiazem), and they depress sinus node automaticity and slow AV conduction. DHPs are more potent vasodilators, cause reflex tachycardia and positive inotropy, do not affect the rate of recovery, and do not affect AV nodal conduction.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Which CCBs are used for hypertensive emergency, and how do they differ?", "back": "Nicardipine and clevidipine by slow I.V. infusion, usually combined with labetalol or esmolol to prevent reflex tachycardia. Nicardipine is potent and long-acting, duration 8 hours or less; clevidipine has a 1-minute half-life and a duration of only 5-15 minutes.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "What is the first-line drug class for variant (vasospastic) angina, and which class must be avoided?", "back": "CCBs are first-line and may be combined with nitroglycerin. Beta blockers should not be used for the vasospastic type because they do not work. In exertional angina the reverse is true: beta blockers are first-line.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Which CCB is used for subarachnoid hemorrhage, and why that one?", "back": "Nimodipine. It is lipophilic and penetrates the CNS, producing cerebral artery vasodilation, which reduces the incidence and severity of ischemic deficits. It is oral only, bioavailability about 13%, half-life 1-2 hours.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "What is the tocolytic use of nifedipine, and what is the real goal of the 48 hours?", "back": "Nifedipine for 48 hours only suppresses premature contractions in preterm labor at 24-34 weeks. The goal is not to reach term but to buy time to give a glucocorticoid for fetal lung maturation and to transfer the mother to a hospital with a neonatal ICU.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Explain the mechanism of dihydropyridine-induced peripheral edema.", "back": "Vasodilation causes pre-capillary (arteriolar) dilation with post-capillary reflex constriction, raising capillary pressure and permeability so fluid redistributes into the interstitium. Because DHPs act on arteries and arterioles and not on venules or veins, this is not volume overload and diuretics will not improve it.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why does verapamil raise digoxin levels?", "back": "Verapamil blocks P-glycoprotein, and digoxin is a P-glycoprotein substrate. Less digoxin is exported into the gut and the renal tubule, so systemic levels rise. Digoxin has a very narrow therapeutic index, so the result is digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia risk.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "By what two mechanisms do beta blockers lower blood pressure?", "back": "Blocking cardiac beta-1 receptors slows heart rate, AV nodal conduction and myocardial contractility, reducing cardiac output; blocking renal beta-1 receptors at the juxtaglomerular apparatus inhibits renin release, reducing RAAS activation.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Name the three generations of beta blocker with an example of each.", "back": "First generation, classical non-selective: propranolol (prototype). Second generation, beta-1 selective: metoprolol; esmolol has a 9-minute half-life for infusion in crisis. Third generation, additional actions: carvedilol and labetalol (alpha-1 blockade), nebivolol (nitric oxide production).", "tag": "Glossary" },
    { "front": "What extra vasodilating mechanisms do the third-generation beta blockers have?", "back": "Nitric oxide production (nebivolol, carteolol); beta-2 receptor agonism (carteolol); alpha-1 receptor antagonism (carvedilol, labetalol); calcium entry blockade (carvedilol, betaxolol); antioxidant activity (carvedilol). All of them add vasodilation on top of beta blockade.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Which beta blockers are proven to reduce mortality in stable heart failure?", "back": "Bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol and nebivolol. Bucindolol did not show the effect. Beta blockers must be started at low dose in stable patients because they can precipitate acute decompensation of cardiac function.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why are beta blockers dangerous in a patient on insulin?", "back": "Beta-2 blockade inhibits hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, delaying recovery from hypoglycemia, and beta blockade blunts the adrenergic warning symptoms of tremor, tachycardia and nervousness, so the patient may not recognize a falling glucose. Beta-1 selective agents may be less prone, but selectivity is dose-dependent.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "What is beta blocker withdrawal syndrome and how is it prevented?", "back": "Chronic blockade upregulates beta adrenoceptors, so abrupt discontinuation exposes an enlarged receptor pool to catecholamines: nervousness, tachycardia, blood pressure above pre-treatment levels, increased angina, and increased risk of sudden death. Prevent it by tapering over a week or more.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why can topical beta blocker eye drops cause bronchospasm?", "back": "Drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct and reaches the systemic circulation. Even a small amount can achieve systemic levels sufficient to provoke an asthma attack or adverse cardiovascular effects in susceptible patients.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why are beta blockers no longer first-line for uncomplicated hypertension?", "back": "Diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, CCBs, beta blockers and alpha blockers are essentially equivalent in blood-pressure-lowering effect. Beta blockers lost first-line status with the JNC8 recommendations because of side effects, not because of weaker efficacy; they remain adjunctive and are preferred when a comorbidity such as ischemic heart disease or heart failure exists.", "tag": "Concept" }
